  "question": {
    "text": "An iron rocket fragment initially at<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> enters the earth's atmosphere almost horizontally and quickly fuses completely in atmospheric friction. Specific heat of iron is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kcal</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi>kg</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>°</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>, its melting point is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1535</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mo>°</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>&nbsp;and the latent heat of fusion is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kcal</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi>kg</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>. The minimum velocity with which the fragment must have entered the atmosphere is",
